{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>You shall not sacrifice upon leavened bread the blood of My sacrifice</b> — You shall not slaughter the Paschal offering on the fourteenth of Nisan until you have eliminated the leaven. This is the language of Rashi. But you should not understand from him that he intends to derive from this a prohibition against leaven that must take effect before the time of slaughter, as this is mentioned in the first chapter of Pesaḥim (5a): 'The Merciful One states the time of slaughter' — for this is not the essence of the Scriptural derivation according to the halakhic conclusion there, since eliminating leaven on the eve of Pesach involves no biblical prohibition, not even to eat it. Rather, according to the substance of the matter as it is in halakhah, it is a prohibition to slaughter the Paschal offering while leaven is present. Its meaning is that no leaven should belong to any member of the group registered for that Paschal offering at the time of slaughter. And thus Rashi writes in parashat Ki Tissa (Rashi on Exodus 34:25). Now, it would have been fitting for the verse to say 'You shall not sacrifice upon leavened bread My sacrifice,' since the blood is not sacrificed. But according to our Rabbis (in the Mekhilta here), the word includes the sprinkling as well — that one must not sprinkle upon leavened bread. It says: you shall not slaughter upon leavened bread, and [it says] 'nor the blood of My sacrifice,' meaning: nor shall the blood of My sacrifice be upon leavened bread. And the verse is elliptical."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:31.225551396Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}